TypeElement.name and valueType default to "" — five kinds stop pinning target type names

Dev's framing: it is in the essential nature of a Condition to be Boolean, so that
belongs in the target-language-neutral logic of the Generator, not pinned per instance
in the model — and there are other instances of the same thing.

WHY THEY WERE PINNED: because they had to be. `name` and `valueType` were the only two
ABSTRACT members on TypeElement; `parameters`, `body` and `value` already carried
defaults. Any kind whose type is fixed by the kind itself had to supply a constant to
satisfy the trait, and the constant it supplied was a SCALA TYPE NAME sitting in the
neutral model. Giving the odd two defaults makes them consistent with the other three
and removes the reason:

  Condition   name = ""   valueType = "Boolean"
  Monadic     name = ""   valueType = "Unit"
  Pattern     name = ""   valueType = "draco.rete.Knowledge => Unit"
  Action      name = ""   valueType = {"->": [draco.rete.RhsContext, "Unit"]}
  Factory     name = ""

All gone. Factory KEEPS its valueType — that one comes from _valueType and is
load-bearing (`ActorType` is what tells the projection to mint an actor rather than an
instance); only its name pin went.

CHECKED BEFORE EMPTYING, since a field that something reads would fail silently rather
than loudly: every Monadic emission path in Generator uses `mo.value` alone —
methodBody, typeBody, factoryBody, globalElementsDeclaration — and never valueType.
Condition's, Pattern's and Action's are read nowhere; conditionFunctions hardcodes
`: Boolean` in the signature itself. The generic `te.valueType` fallbacks only walk
dracoAspect.elements, where none of these kinds ever appears.

CORPUS: 97 instances across 26 files lose a valueType the decoder has always ignored
and the encoder will no longer emit — 59 Monadic "Unit", 30 Action "Unit", 8 Condition
"Boolean". That also settles an inconsistency the corpus already had: rule JSONs carried
an ignored `"valueType": "Unit"` on their actions but nothing on their patterns, because
one was authored and the other was not.

Pattern.drake needed removing by hand rather than by the same sweep as the others: its
pin was "draco.rete.Knowledge => Unit" with a Scala arrow INSIDE A STRING LITERAL, which
is why yesterday's arrow conversion correctly left it alone — quoted text is data, not
drake syntax.

NAMED, NOT DONE — the rest of what the survey found. A group of Generator helpers is
already language-neutral and already written, but lives inside the Scala engine, so a
second target would duplicate it or depend on Scala for it: isDomain / isRule / isActor
/ isObjectOnly / isLeaf and the role-aspect count (classifying a definition),
conditionParameters, isActorMintingFactory, inheritedElementNames /
findDiscriminatedParent / collectLeafModules / moduleOrder (derivation-graph walks and
the topological sort), the codec-derivation gate. Separate from those is the genuine
slot boundary — neutral idea, Scala spelling — where nullValueFor ("the zero of a type"
vs ""/Seq.empty/0/false) and elisionCheck ("omit an empty field" vs
`if (x.nonEmpty) Some(…) else None`) sit. Extraction is a placement question staged
toward Source (#57), left for Dev to call.

Also left: `Boolean` and `Unit` themselves are still target spellings wherever a target
supplies them. The neutral form of those is the primitive set, which is #51's, frozen —
and Dev's direction for it is wrap types, one tier below draco.rete's wrapping of host
library types, gated on derivationRef emitting parents bare.
EOF

